00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.957
Analysts are paid to doubt confident numbers.

2
00:00:03.958 --> 00:00:13.013
So when an LLM started reading deal documents for private-equity investors, the danger wasn't that it would be wrong.

3
00:00:13.014 --> 00:00:15.759
It was that it would be fluent.

4
00:00:16.846 --> 00:00:20.343
It refused to launch as a naked score.

5
00:00:20.344 --> 00:00:29.750
From day one, every claim carried its source — and thin evidence drew an honest, I'm not sure, instead of a guess.

6
00:00:29.751 --> 00:00:31.896
A score is a claim.

7
00:00:31.897 --> 00:00:36.833
Experts don't buy claims — they buy claims with references.

8
00:00:36.834 --> 00:00:41.505
No score travels alone: the drawer is the proof surface.

9
00:00:41.506 --> 00:00:43.626
Explain this score.

10
00:00:43.627 --> 00:00:54.864
And the number opens into its evidence: the board pack, the audited statements — and the one claim that couldn't be verified, kept visible instead of hidden.

11
00:00:55.990 --> 00:01:07.269
Deal screening ran sixty percent faster, measured before and after rollout — because analysts stopped re-verifying every machine claim by hand.

12
00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:14.534
Lead product designer on a cross-functional team — engineers, data scientists, a PM.

13
00:01:14.535 --> 00:01:23.870
The client is under NDA, so these are labelled reconstructions; the real artifacts are walked through on a call.

14
00:01:24.982 --> 00:01:34.949
Where this breaks: provenance-first pays only when readers actually open the references, and wrong answers are expensive.

15
00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:38.672
Citations can't save a guessing model.

16
00:01:38.673 --> 00:01:43.067
For expert skeptics, provenance is the product.

